Razors ultra thin blade laptops are  generally considered to be in a league  of their own when it comes to industrial  design they are the most powerful or  most affordable gaming laptops in the  market but the hardware that razors  engineers managed to cram into such a  sleek chassis without damming it to  overheat is a commendable feat the  exterior looks nearly identical to past  blades but why mess with a good thing  razor whether intentionally or not has  created a gaming laptop that resembles  Apple's MacBook laptops I'll be it with  a modest amount of gaming Flair a Razer  logo here and some green lights they're  the only tells that this is a product  meant for gamers but that's a good thing  the black anodized aluminum chassis  feels a lot better than the plastics  used for most gaming laptops and it  gives the blade an air of class that  most gaming products lack the latest  1080p version of the blade is priced at  $2,000 packing a gtx 970m mobile GPU  from Nvidia with three gigabytes of RAM  and a quad core intel core i7 processor  with a maximum clock speed of 3.6  gigahertz while not the pinnacle of  portable gaming tech the full HD blade  packs enough power to run most of  today's most demanding games at a decent  framerate with max or near max settings  BioShock Infinite ran really smooth at  Ultra settings hitting an average of 88  frames per second while running the  built-in benchmarking tool with tress-fx  enabled Tomb Raider can be a demanding  game but the blade was able to handle  tress-fx Ultra settings and FX AAA while  generating an average frame rate of 55  frames per second with a few small  compromises it's a cinch to get it  running above 60 frames per second Metro  last light is particularly challenging  even for the strongest gaming race we  had to disable a few advanced features  including tessellation super sampling  anti-aliasing and advanced physics but  we were still able to hit 60 frames per  second on average with ultra settings  battlefield 4 on office settings start  pretty well with Forex MSAA  averaging 55 frames per second during  gameplay while it's not a game unit in  heaven is a reliable benchmarking tool  designed to push hardware to the limit  with ultra settings including extreme  tessellation razor's blade fared quite  well averaging 34 frames per second  during our test the new 1080p blade  holds up really well under real-world  conditions while our current test proved  to be a capable gaming machine today  the three gigabytes of gddr5 vram will  be the first weak link in the chain as  PC games continue down the road of  larger textures and graphical bells and  whistles what's worse you aren't able to  upgrade the eight gigabytes of system  ram 8 gigabytes is a good amount for now  but not being able to install Ram may  prove to be a thorn in the side of some  people down the road other manufacturers  make it easy to swap RAM in and out of a  laptop but in order to keep the blade  Finn razor had to solder its memory  chips directly to its proprietary  motherboard of course there's also  something to be said for the blades  simple selection of connectivity ports  with only three USB ports HDMI out in a  combo headphone microphone port it may  leave some users wanting the lack of an  Ethernet port is the biggest issue we  faced as it made downloading today's  large games a longer process than it  should have been  while it's got a good Wi-Fi radio we  eventually reached for an Ethernet to  USB adapter for such a thin laptop the  blades battery lasts a reasonable amount  of time while it's untethered from  external power source we were able to  keep the blade alive for nearly four  hours while gaming and if you're keen to  use it for non gaming activities with a  slightly dim screen expect that number  to rise considerably as the Intel HD GPU  that lies within the core i7 CPU will  draw far less power than the discrete  NVIDIA GPU with a 256 gigabyte SSD the  blade can move a large amount of data  with ease but it doesn't necessarily  hold a lot of data a 512 gigabyte drive  would alleviate some of the space  constraints that occur when installing  multiple games to the blade but  unfortunately razer doesn't offer such  options on the full HD model it only  comes with 256 gigabytes so the new  blade is a capable portable and great  looking gaming laptop but more than ever  the blade is facing competition from  outsiders as is usual with razor's  laptops the decision to choose a blade  over something from the competition  comes down your appreciation for its  high quality chassis if you choose the  blade with all of its limitations in  mind you won't be disappointed it's not  the cheapest option around but it's not  always about comparing specs and numbers  when build quality is factored in here  the blade is the slickest gaming laptop  money can buy
 

